# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Flutter's web output has no content hashes in it: every build ships the same
# main.dart.wasm, main.dart.js, canvaskit/ and assets/ URLs as the last one.
# There is therefore no filename a browser can use to tell two builds apart,
# and anything cached without revalidation is cached until it is evicted — a
# nursery tablet would keep last term's app however often it was reloaded.
#
# So: one rule, covering everything, requiring a conditional request before
# any stored copy is reused. The cost is an ETag round trip, and even that is
# rare, because sw.js serves the app out of the Cache API and only goes to the
# network when a new build is being installed.
#
# /sw.js is the one that really matters. It is how a new build is noticed at
# all, so it must never be answered from a cache.
#
# Long-lived immutable caching would be the usual answer here, and it is the
# wrong one for this output: with no hash in the name, the URL that would be
# pinned for a year is the same URL the next deploy publishes to.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
